So \code{refresh = TRUE} always keeps the ID (links and saved searches keep
working). Only when the collection is gone on OpenAlex, or holds another
entity, is a new one created.

\strong{Local folder gone:} a collection with the same name that exists in
your account but is not recorded in \code{project_folder} is never deleted.
Without \code{refresh}, \code{pro_collection()} stops. With \code{refresh = TRUE} it takes
that collection over: the ID stays, its members are synced to the query
as above, and the folder is written again. Names match ignoring case, and
the collection must hold the same entity.

\strong{Costs:} creating and managing collections and downloading the member
IDs are free. An import costs the same credits as paging through the
results yourself (one list request per 200 results). Imports run at about
200 results per second (measured November 2026), so a collection of the
maximum 1,000,000 members takes over an hour. Interrupting \code{pro_collection()}
does not stop the import on the server; running it again waits for it.

\strong{Several queries in one collection:} OpenAlex imports a query of at
most 20,000 characters of OQL (about 600 to 800 DOIs); a longer single
query stops before anything is created. For long ID or DOI lists, split
them into chunks, e.g. \code{pro_query(doi = dois, chunk_limit = 200)}, and pass
the list with \code{name}. The queries are then not imported: they are fetched
with \code{\link[=pro_fetch]{pro_fetch()}} (\code{select = "id"}, in parallel with \code{workers}) and the
IDs are added to the collection (\code{member_ids}, 10,000 per request, free).
This costs the same credits as imports and was about five times faster
with \code{workers = 8} (3,000 DOIs: 8.5 s instead of 45 to 55 s, November
2026). Fields in \code{select} beyond \code{id} are fetched in the same pass, from
the queries themselves, so the records do not depend on the collection
filter limit. An interrupted run resumes the fetch and syncs the members
again (syncing is idempotent). A changed query re-creates the collection
unless \code{refresh = TRUE}, which keeps it, fetches everything again and
syncs the members.

\strong{Fetch first:} the queries are fetched \emph{before} the collection is
created, so a query that fails leaves no empty collection behind. This is
always the route for a list with \code{name}; \code{fetch_first = TRUE} takes it for
single queries too (and for each element of a list without \code{name})
instead of an import. OpenAlex does not check that the IDs it is given
exist, but these come from the queries, so they do.

\strong{Limits:} 1,000,000 members per collection, 500 collections per
account, names up to 100 characters and unique per account. A collection
with more than 300,000 members (100,000 for authors) cannot be used as a
filter, so its records cannot be downloaded with \code{select}; its member IDs
can. Queries with \code{group_by} or \code{sample} cannot be imported.

\strong{Core and expansion works:} a collection holds all its members, and the
member IDs in \verb{members/} include expansion works (DataCite, repositories).
A \emph{filter} by a works collection returns only its core works unless
\code{corpus = "all"} is set, so the records in \verb{records/} are fetched with
\code{corpus = "all"}, and later queries should set it too:
\code{pro_query(collection = id, options = list(corpus = "all"))}. Whether
expansion works get into the collection depends on the query that fills
it (\code{options = list(corpus = "all")} in \code{\link[=pro_query]{pro_query()}}).

\strong{API keys:} collections need a \emph{personal} OpenAlex API key
(\code{api_key}); organisation keys and some older keys are refused
(HTTP 401) even when they work for queries. The queries that fill the
collection can run with another key (\code{api_key_fetch}, e.g. one with a
larger daily budget); they cost credits, the collection calls do not.
Records downloaded through the collection filter (the import route) use
\code{api_key}: a private collection is visible only to its owner's
key (another key gets HTTP 404).
